<p>Note, we use <code>intervals1[i]</code> and <code>intervals2[j]</code> i=
nstead of A and B since we don=E2=80=99t know which interval array A or B b=
elongs to.</p>
<hr>
<p>We=E2=80=99ve now identified a process that allows us to identify and re=
cord all overlaps while traversing the arrays of intervals. For the pair of=
 intervals being considered at <code>i</code> and <code>j</code>:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>Set A as the interval that starts first, and B as the other interval.</p=
>
</li>
<li>
<p>Check if <code>A.end =E2=89=A5 B.start</code> to see if these intervals =
overlap. If they do, record the overlap as [<code>B.start</code>, <code>min=
(A.end, B.end)</code>].</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Whichever interval ends first, advance its corresponding pointer to move=
 to the next interval.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>Continue to apply these steps until either <code>i</code> or <code>j</co=
de> have passed the end of their array. Once this happens, we know there wo=
n=E2=80=99t be any more overlapping intervals.</p>
